{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790361392,"nonce":"cFk/SSBB47IF+Pmw//lfu/2l85c=","title":"A with scope left through a conditional branch or a switch keeps its handler frame","message":"`LoweringContext::lower_with_scopes` (crates/compiler/src/lowering.rs) appends `__zyntax_effect_pop_handler`, and the `Intrinsic::Free` of a stateful handler's region, to every scope block whose terminator returns or branches to a block outside the scope. A `CondBranch` with one target inside the scope and one outside gets no pop on the outside edge: the pass logs \"mixed in/out CondBranch in handler scope\" and moves on. `Switch` terminators are not classified at all. On those edges the frame stays on the thread's handler stack after the scope has ended, so a later perform resolves to a handler that is out of scope, and the handler's state region is never freed.\n\nWhat exists: the scope's block set (`typed_cfg::WithScopeInfo::body_blocks`), the pop and free sequence, and the same exit-edge question answered separately (and more narrowly) by `emit_fiber_drops` for fibers.\n\nDone: every edge that leaves a `with` body, including one arm of a conditional branch and any switch case, runs the pop and the free exactly once, through an edge-split block. One exit-edge emitter serves both handler pops and fiber drops so the two cannot cover different edges. A test leaves a `with` scope through each terminator shape and checks `handler_stack_depth()` returns to its baseline.","files":null}]}